            <fr:anchor>962</fr:anchor>
            <fr:addr type="user">sss-000W</fr:addr>
            <fr:route>sss-000W.xml</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Deterministic Regular Expressions">Deterministic Regular Expressions</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <fr:p><fr:em>Deterministic regular expressions</fr:em> are a grammar formalism for defining regular expressions that correspond to deterministic regular languages.

In the spirit of <fr:link href="bruggemannkleinwood.xml" title="Deterministic regular languages" addr="bruggemannkleinwood" type="local">Bruggemann-Klein and Wood</fr:link> and <fr:link href="krishnaswami_typed_2019.xml" title="A typed, algebraic approach to parsing" addr="krishnaswami_typed_2019" type="local">Krishaswami and Yallop</fr:link>, inductively define the deterministic regular expressions in terms of their <fr:link href="sss-0014.xml" title="Nullable Grammar" addr="sss-0014" type="local">nullability</fr:link>, <fr:link href="sss-000Y.xml" title="First Set" addr="sss-000Y" type="local">first set</fr:link>, and <fr:link href="sss-0010.xml" title="FollowLast Set" addr="sss-0010" type="local">followlast set</fr:link>:
  <fr:ul><fr:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\epsilon ]]></fr:tex>, the grammar accepting only the empty string.</fr:li>
    <fr:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\texttt {"}c\texttt {"}]]></fr:tex>, the grammar only accepting the length 1 string comprising the character <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> for some character <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> drawn from the alphabet</fr:li>
    <fr:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r \otimes  r']]></fr:tex>, the concatenation of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r']]></fr:tex> where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\neg  \mathsf {Null}(r)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(r) \cap  \mathsf {First}(r') = \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></fr:li>
    <fr:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r \oplus  r']]></fr:tex>, the disjunction of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r']]></fr:tex> where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\neg  (\mathsf {Null}(r) \land  \mathsf {Null}(r'))]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {First}(r) \cap  \mathsf {First}(r') = \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></fr:li>
    <fr:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[{r}^{*}]]></fr:tex>, the Kleene star of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex> where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\neg  \mathsf {Null}(r)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(r) \cap  \mathsf {First}(r) = \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></fr:li></fr:ul></fr:p>
            <fr:p>In each of the above constructors, the constraints on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {First}(r)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(r)]]></fr:tex> remove sources of ambiguity in a regular expressions. They provide a sound guarantee that the resulting regular expression has at most one parse for any string.</fr:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
