            <fr:anchor>963</fr:anchor>
            <fr:addr type="user">sss-000Z</fr:addr>
            <fr:route>sss-000Z.xml</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="First Sets in Dependent Lambek Calculus">First Sets in Dependent Lambek Calculus</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <fr:p>The <fr:link href="sss-000Y.xml" title="First Set" addr="sss-000Y" type="local">first set</fr:link> of a grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> may be captured in Dependent Lambek Calculus via the following proposition:

  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ c \not  \in  \mathsf {First}(A) := A \And  (\texttt {"}c\texttt {"} \otimes  \top ) \vdash  \bot ]]></fr:tex></fr:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
              <fr:month>1</fr:month>
              <fr:day>24</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:anchor>964</fr:anchor>
            <fr:addr type="user">sss-0017</fr:addr>
            <fr:route>sss-0017.xml</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Sequential Unambiguity">Sequential Unambiguity</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <fr:p>Grammars <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex> are <fr:em>sequentially unambiguous</fr:em> if the <fr:link href="sss-0010.xml" title="FollowLast Set" addr="sss-0010" type="local">followlast set</fr:link> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is disjoint from the <fr:link href="sss-000Y.xml" title="First Set" addr="sss-000Y" type="local">first set</fr:link> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>.

<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(A) \cap  \mathsf {First}(B) = \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></fr:p>
            <fr:p>We can understand this intuitively by characterizing the behavior of a left-to-right parser of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A \otimes  B]]></fr:tex>. First it searches for a parse of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>, then upon finding a character that is not in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(A)]]></fr:tex> it may begin trying search for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>.

That is, there is a unique boundary between the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>-parse and the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>-parse.</fr:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
